imagine you have some kind of voice  within you they try to ask yourself am i  behaving morally or not that's voice  sometimes is asleep hi I'm Mamie  Rockwell for recent TV and I'm here at  the games for Change Conference at the  Tribeca Film Festival here with Dan  Ariely who is a behavioral economist at  Duke University and the author of the  recent book the honest truth about  dishonesty how we lie to everyone and  especially ourselves so it's lovely to  chat with you today Sam you um this by  the way might have been one of your  lives for today which we often do we  tell white lies to people it may or may  not be lovely to chat with you stay who  knows so tell me a little bit about this  contraption that you've built here this  is called a truth box and you built it  for the conference tell me how this  works so this is basically give you a  chance for confession so what we ask  people to go inside and to say to tell  about a lie that was meaningful in their  lives something that they've learned  from thought about and so on and we've  had some fantastic stories about people  doing all kinds of things and you know  we have this this belief that yes we're  honest and it's other people but but  having to face some of your own past and  some of the lies that you have told that  might have been very uncomfortable and  you can tell a story about this but why  was it interesting and so on it's  actually a very interesting process for  us as researchers and I think also  cleansing and useful for the people who  are participating in it everyone  considers themselves to be an honest  person but there is that underlying  conflict that people have between  telling the truth and then wanting to  cut corners and get ahead so which side  do you find usually wins out so so  here's the thing there's lots of ways to  lie and the odd thing is that we lie a  lot and we think of ourselves as good  people and here's a here's one study it  imagine two people come to a room they  don't know each other and you say  introduce yourself to the other person  talked about yourself for 10 minutes at  the end of the 10 minutes you put them  in different rooms and you say did you  lie  and everybody said me of course not then  they say luckily we taped everything  you've said let's play it back to you  sentence by sentence and tell us whether  each sentence is absolutely the truth or  not and at the end of this exercise  people admit to lying two to three times  in ten minutes and think about it in  social interactions do you really want  to tell people everything you think  about them do you really want to portray  yourself in the most objective way so  the reality is that we lie a lot and we  recognize it and much of it foe is for  social lubrication much of it is white  lies and but it sometimes also happens  in there in the workplace well I mean  you did mention in your book that not  everyone is like a Bernie Madoff not  everyone is gonna try and get you for  all you're worth but everyone tends to  lie a little bit and those lies  accumulate and they actually can be  quite destructive so what are some ways  that you suggest that we can mitigate  these lives we can actually decrease the  amount of cheating and lying in our own  lives and in the lives of others so  first of all it turns out that having  strict rules really helped imagine the  tax code being very gray or very clear  and I think about something like  alcoholic anonymous and they have a rule  that says no drinking whatsoever what  would happen if the rule was half a  glass a day max right the glasses will  become very big you would say I'm not  drinking today I'll drink twice tomorrow  there'll be all kinds of negotiations  and not because we want to cheat other  people is because we tell ourselves  stories that are convenient for us in  the short term the other thing is having  some moral reminders so we do lots of  experiments we tempt people to steal  money from us get them to do a little  test and then they gray themselves and  they get paid by how much they gray  themselves but we can find out if they  greater themselves extra positively  compared to what they did but if we do  experiments in which we ask people to  first try to recall the Ten Commandments  or swear on the Bible or all kinds of  things like that  cheating goes down dramatically  sometimes is eliminated so if you think  about it imagine you have some kind of  voice within you they tries to ask  yourself am i behaving morally or not  that voice sometimes is asleep allow you  to do all kinds of things but if you  just recited Ten Commandments talked  about morality and so on officer and you  behave better  of the things we find is a pattern we  call the what the hell effect and the  idea is yeah people people kind of kind  of cheat a little bit cheat a little bit  and at some point they switch and start  cheating all the time and and people  report on this in dieting so let's say  you have a strict diet and you start the  morning with a muffin you say today I'm  not a diet here I might as well enjoy it  and the logic is that we think of  ourselves in binary terms were either  good or bad now if you're 92% good you  can still think of yourself as a good  person but if you're 78% good is it  really worthwhile moving to 80 82  maybe not you might as well go all the  way and enjoy it so we did some  experiments with confession it's a non  Catholic a confession and we found that  confession really helps so the moment  you declare what you've done badly you  are able to start a new page and you can  think about the Truth and Reconciliation  Act in South Africa is opening a new  page how do you go from apartheid to  post apartheid there's no smooth  transition but if you stand there and  you say here's all the bad things we've  done and tomorrow we'll try to open a  new page this really helps and I think  we need to think about more mechanisms  for that and then the most important  thing is eliminating conflicts of  interest if you like a particular soccer  team and the referee is calling a call  against your team you can't help I think  of the referees evil vicious stupid  blind something like that because you  want to see things in a certain way so  what happened is that we have a  motivated cognition we're able to see we  have tremendous flexibility in how we  can see the world and if we have a  conflicts of interest we will see the  world in a very different way lobbying  turns out to be incredibly valuable now  why because people are cheap and what I  mean is that you can buy somebody a  sandwich in a drink and all of a sudden  they start seeing life from your  perspective do you find that there are  certain types of people who are more  honest than others so in that respect  I'd probably say that you know you've  got politicians up here who are  dishonest and perhaps nuns and firemen  down here do you see late lying as being  universal so so in general we find it to  be universal we've tried first of all we  tried many countries so we tried China  in Israel we tried Italy we tried  England of course we tried Canada the  Canadians always think they are better  they are not a  tried South Africa we tried Columbia we  tried many countries we don't find many  differences and we also don't find  difference with intelligence with  risk-taking the one thing we do find is  that creative people cheat more and  here's the story imagines it's all about  rationalization you want to look at  yourself in the mirror and feel good  about it you also want to benefit from  cheating and you cheat a little bit so  you can cheat and benefit from it but  still look on yourself in the mirror and  feel good about yourself it's all about  telling a story and if you're creative  you can tell by the stories which means  you can cheat a little bit more and  still feel good about yourself

